  "contentHtml": "\n                                    \n\n                                                                            <p><img src=\"https://health.economictimes.indiatimes.com/Themes/Release/theme4/images/icons/et-default-user.svg\" alt=\"Anuja Jaiswal\">\n                                            \n                                        </p>\n                                                                        \n                                                                                    <ul>\n                                                                                                    <li>\n                                                                                                                                                                            <a>Anuja Jaiswal</a>\n                                                            \n                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        \n                                                                                                            </li>\n                                                                                                                                                    <li><a href=\"/agency/87654367/TNN\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TNN</a></li>\n                                                                                            </ul>\n                                                                                <ul>\n                                                                                            <li>Published On Oct 11, 2026 at 07:08 PM IST</li>\n                                                                                                                                </ul>\n                                    \n                                                                            \n                                                                        \n                                \n                                \n\n                    <!-- Display video article in news article for the custom next story -->\n                    \n                    <!-- Modifying the body placing embeds,promoslots,related etc.-->\n                                        <figure><img src=\"/images/default.jpg\" alt=\"\"></figure><p>New <a href=\"/tag/delhi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Delhi</a>: Patients as young as 15 are being seen at a Delhi hospital with gastrointestinal (GI) cancer, prompting experts to call for closer investigation into environmental exposures, including <a href=\"/tag/air+pollution\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">air pollution</a>, alongside established risk factors such as diet, lifestyle and genetic susceptibility.Experts highlighted the concern at a two-day international conference on colorectal and other GI cancers, being held on Oct 10 and 11 and organised by <a href=\"/tag/sir+ganga+ram+hospital\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Sir Ganga Ram Hospital</a> in collaboration with The Christie NHS Foundation Trust, UK.</p><!-- PROMOSLOT_M --><p>They discussed emerging evidence linking air pollution to cancer risk, while stressing the need for India-specific research to determine whether pollutants contribute to GI cancers.</p>\n                            <p>Advt\n                            </p>\n\n                              \n                                                         \n                        <p>“In our OPD we are now seeing new GI cancer patients as young as 15, which was uncommon five years ago,” said <a href=\"/tag/dr+shyam+aggarwal\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dr Shyam Aggarwal</a>, chairperson of medical oncology at Sir Ganga Ram Hospital and organising chairperson of the conference.</p><!-- PROMOSLOT_M --><p>A 2026 review of 70 studies on air pollution and gastrointestinal diseases found that long-term exposure to fine particulate matter (<a href=\"/tag/pm2.5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">PM2.5</a>) was associated with higher mortality from GI cancers, with a notable association involving <a href=\"/tag/colorectal+cancer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">colorectal cancer</a>.A separate study published in <em>Nature Health</em> examined links between long-term exposure to PM2.5, <a href=\"/tag/nitrogen+dioxide\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">nitrogen dioxide</a> and <a href=\"/tag/ozone\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ozone</a>, and overall cancer risk. These findings indicate associations, not proof that the pollutants directly cause GI cancers.</p><!-- PROMOSLOT_M --><p>The Indian picture also calls for closer investigation. An analysis of trends in England and India cited an early-onset colorectal cancer rate of 8.4 per 1 lakh among people under 50 in England in 2023, compared with 3.5 per 1 lakh among Indians aged 25-49. Indian urban cancer registries have shown a steady increase in colorectal cancer, while hospital studies have reported younger patients being diagnosed at advanced stages.</p><!-- PROMOSLOT_M --><p><a href=\"/tag/dr+saumitra+rawat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Dr Saumitra Rawat</a>, chairperson of surgical gastroenterology and GI and HPB onco-surgery at Sir Ganga Ram Hospital, said younger GI cancer patients should be assessed for multiple possible contributors, including family history, genetic susceptibility, diet, lifestyle, infections and environmental exposures.</p>\n                            <p>Advt\n                            </p>\n\n                              \n                                                         \n                        <p>Experts called for stronger air-quality monitoring, improved cancer registries and long-term Indian studies examining the link between pollution exposure and clinical outcomes. They also stressed the need for timely medical consultation for persistent digestive symptoms, along with established cancer-prevention measures, appropriate screening and early diagnosis.                    </p><!-- Category Most Read Placement -->\n                                                                    \n                                                                \n                                <ul>\n                                                                            <!-- <li>By <span>Anuja Jaiswal</span>,</li> -->\n                                    \n\n                                                                            <li>By\n                                                                                            <a>Anuja Jaiswal</a>\n\n                                                                                                                                                                                        , \n                                                                                    </li>\n                                                                        <li><a href=\"/agency/87654367/TNN\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">TNN</a></li>\n                                </ul>\n                                <ul>\n                                    <!-- <li> Updated On Oct 11, 2026 at 07:08 PM IST</li> -->\n                                    <li>Published On Oct 11, 2026 at 07:08 PM IST</li>\n                                    <!-- <li class=\"desktop-view\">2 min read</li> -->\n                                </ul>\n                            \n                                        \n\n                    \n\n                    <!-- Comment Section -->\n                                            \n                                        <!-- Comment Section -->\n\n        